{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y5qVmeZTJwS9c7XeWV5WAt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Major reframe required — from ''solving a current practical problem''\n2\t  to ''identifying and formalizing a latent bias mechanism with demonstrated consequences.''\n3\t  Three-move pivot: (1) Lead with bias identity as primary theoretical contribution\n4\t  (R²=0.99/0.97, holds regardless of LLM quality). (2) Demonstrate mechanism severity\n5\t  on controlled benchmarks (synthetic 2-3x, CausalForest 5.9x); argue magnitude scales\n6\t  with annotator error heterogeneity — current SOTA on easy English CSS is a floor,\n7\t  not a ceiling. (3) Reframe EC-HTE as ''correct correction framework when mechanism\n8\t  activates.'' Drop diagnostic from main contributions → appendix. Add ''Characterizing\n9\t  the Homogeneity Boundary'' analysis. Real-data section becomes ''Audit of Current\n10\t  Practice'' confirming theory predicts when correction is unnecessary. Target: EMNLP\n11\t  Findings.\n12\t\n13\t  '\n14\timagined_experiments:\n15\t- name: Real-data case study — UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\n16\t  plan_id: exp-004\n17\t  description: 'Apply EC-HTE to UC Berkeley hate speech corpus with demographic subgroups\n18\t    (race, gender, age, education). Annotate with GPT-4.1 via API. Compare global-corrected\n19\t    vs EC-HTE CATE estimates. Frame as ''Audit of Current Practice'' — show that when\n20\t    LLM errors are homogeneous, global and EC-HTE converge, confirming theory predicts\n21\t    when correction is (un)necessary.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    llm_overall_accuracy: 0.82\n26\t    llm_delta_pi_max_across_demographic_subgroups: 0.03\n27\t    llm_delta_pi_race: 0.02\n28\t    llm_delta_pi_gender: 0.01\n29\t    llm_delta_pi_age: 0.04\n30\t    llm_delta_pi_education: 0.03\n31\t    global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_abs: 0.018\n32\t    global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_se: 0.35\n33\t    global_vs_ec_hte_mean_cate_shift_se: 0.18\n34\t    num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_1se: 0\n35\t    num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_0_5se: 1\n36\t    ec_hte_vs_global_qualitative_agreement: true\n37\t    bootstrap_ci_overlap_pct: 100\n38\t    domain_knowledge_consistency: both methods agree on direction of demographic effects\n39\t    expert_labels_used: 500\n40\t    n_total_llm_annotated: 39565\n41\t  result_rationale: 'GPT-4.1 achieves uniformly high accuracy on English hate speech\n42\t    classification — a well-studied CSS task with abundant training data. Demographic\n43\t    subgroups (race, gender, age, education of annotator or target) produce Δπ ≤ 0.04,\n44\t    well below the threshold where the bias mechanism becomes practically relevant.\n45\t    With near-homogeneous confusion matrices across subgroups, the global pooled CM\n46\t    is an adequate approximation of each subgroup CM, so global correction and EC-HTE\n47\t    produce statistically indistinguishable CATE estimates. All point estimate shifts\n48\t    fall within 0.35 SE — not actionable. This is exactly the prediction of the bias\n49\t    identity: when Δπ ≈ 0, bias ∝ Δπ×Δτ ≈ 0 regardless of CATE heterogeneity. The\n50\t    result is a negative finding for practical relevance but a positive finding for\n51\t    theory validation — the framework correctly identifies the homogeneous regime\n52\t    as safe for global correction.\n53\t\n54\t    '\n55\t  tables:\n56\t  - name: 'Table: Real-Data CATE Estimates by Demographic Subgroup'\n57\t    columns:\n58\t    - subgroup\n59\t    - n\n60\t    - pi_hat_global\n61\t    - pi_hat_subgroup\n62\t    - cate_naive\n63\t    - cate_global\n64\t    - cate_ec_hte\n65\t    - shift_se\n66\t    - bootstrap_95ci_overlap\n67\t    rows:\n68\t    - - race_white\n69\t      - 18200\n70\t      - 0.17\n71\t      - 0.16\n72\t      - 0.031\n73\t      - 0.028\n74\t      - 0.027\n75\t      - 0.15\n76\t      - true\n77\t    - - race_nonwhite\n78\t      - 21365\n79\t      - 0.17\n80\t      - 0.18\n81\t      - 0.044\n82\t      - 0.04\n83\t      - 0.041\n84\t      - 0.22\n85\t      - true\n86\t    - - gender_male\n87\t      - 20100\n88\t      - 0.17\n89\t      - 0.17\n90\t      - 0.039\n91\t      - 0.035\n92\t      - 0.035\n93\t      - 0.05\n94\t      - true\n95\t    - - gender_female\n96\t      - 19465\n97\t      - 0.17\n98\t      - 0.18\n99\t      - 0.036\n100\t      - 0.033\n101\t      - 0.034\n102\t      - 0.18\n103\t      - true\n104\t    - - age_under30\n105\t      - 15800\n106\t      - 0.17\n107\t      - 0.16\n108\t      - 0.042\n109\t      - 0.038\n110\t      - 0.037\n111\t      - 0.2\n112\t      - true\n113\t    - - age_30plus\n114\t      - 23765\n115\t      - 0.17\n116\t      - 0.19\n117\t      - 0.033\n118\t      - 0.03\n119\t      - 0.031\n120\t      - 0.28\n121\t      - true\n122\t    - - edu_college\n123\t      - 22100\n124\t      - 0.17\n125\t      - 0.17\n126\t      - 0.037\n127\t      - 0.034\n128\t      - 0.034\n129\t      - 0.08\n130\t      - true\n131\t    - - edu_no_college\n132\t      - 17465\n133\t      - 0.17\n134\t      - 0.2\n135\t      - 0.038\n136\t      - 0.034\n137\t      - 0.036\n138\t      - 0.35\n139\t      - true\n140\t  - name: 'Table: Misclassification Heterogeneity Summary'\n141\t    columns:\n142\t    - demographic_dimension\n143\t    - n_subgroups\n144\t    - max_delta_pi\n145\t    - min_pi\n146\t    - max_pi\n147\t    - heterogeneity_regime\n148\t    rows:\n149\t    - - race\n150\t      - 2\n151\t      - 0.02\n152\t      - 0.16\n153\t      - 0.18\n154\t      - homogeneous\n155\t    - - gender\n156\t      - 2\n157\t      - 0.01\n158\t      - 0.17\n159\t      - 0.18\n160\t      - homogeneous\n161\t    - - age\n162\t      - 2\n163\t      - 0.04\n164\t      - 0.16\n165\t      - 0.19\n166\t      - homogeneous\n167\t    - - education\n168\t      - 2\n169\t      - 0.03\n170\t      - 0.17\n171\t      - 0.2\n172\t      - homogeneous\n173\t  figures_description:\n174\t  - 'Scatter plot: global-corrected CATE vs EC-HTE CATE per subgroup, with 45-degree\n175\t    reference line and bootstrap CIs. All points cluster tightly around the identity\n176\t    line (max deviation 0.35 SE), visually confirming no meaningful divergence. Annotate\n177\t    each point with demographic label.'\n178\t  - 'Bar chart: per-subgroup misclassification rate (pi_hat) with 95% CI error bars,\n179\t    grouped by demographic dimension. All bars overlap substantially, visualizing\n180\t    the homogeneous error regime. Horizontal dashed line at global pooled rate.'\n181\t  analysis: 'The real-data audit confirms the worst-tier prediction: on a well-studied\n182\t    English CSS task, SOTA LLMs produce near-homogeneous errors across demographic\n183\t    subgroups (max Δπ = 0.04 for age). Consequently, global and EC-HTE CATE estimates\n184\t    are statistically indistinguishable — the largest shift is 0.35 SE (education\n185\t    dimension), with 100% bootstrap CI overlap across all subgroups. This result has\n186\t    TWO interpretations for the paper: (1) Negative: EC-HTE provides no practical\n187\t    benefit on this dataset, undermining claims of immediate applicability. (2) Positive\n188\t    (reframed): The theory correctly predicts that correction is unnecessary when\n189\t    Δπ ≈ 0, validating the diagnostic framework. Frame as ''the bias identity correctly\n190\t    identifies the homogeneous regime.'' Causal framework mapping: T = exposure to\n191\t    hate speech content, Y = perceived severity rating, Z = hate speech type (LLM-classified),\n192\t    S = demographic subgroup. Note limitation: causal interpretation is illustrative,\n193\t    not designed for rigorous causal inference on this observational corpus. CRITICAL\n194\t    PIVOT: use this result to motivate the ''Characterizing the Homogeneity Boundary''\n195\t    analysis — at what Δπ does the mechanism activate? Back-calculate from the bias\n196\t    identity: for bias > 0.05 (practical threshold), need Δπ > ~0.12 given observed\n197\t    Δτ. This characterization is itself a contribution — it tells practitioners when\n198\t    to worry.\n199\t\n200\t    '\n201\t- name: Multiple LLM annotator comparison\n202\t  plan_id: exp-008\n203\t  description: 'Annotate TweetEval sentiment with GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPU),\n204\t    and Claude Sonnet 4.6 (API). Measure per-subgroup misclassification rates for\n205\t    each LLM. Compare EC-HTE benefit across LLMs of different quality tiers.\n206\t\n207\t    '\n208\t  expected_results:\n209\t    gpt4_1_overall_accuracy: 0.88\n210\t    gpt4_1_delta_pi_max: 0.03\n211\t    gpt4_1_bias_amplification_median: 1.08\n212\t    qwen3_overall_accuracy: 0.81\n213\t    qwen3_delta_pi_max: 0.05\n214\t    qwen3_bias_amplification_median: 1.22\n215\t    claude_sonnet_overall_accuracy: 0.86\n216\t    claude_sonnet_delta_pi_max: 0.04\n217\t    claude_sonnet_bias_amplification_median: 1.14\n218\t    ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_gpt4_1_n500_k4: 2.1\n219\t    ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_qwen3_n500_k4: 1.7\n220\t    ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_claude_n500_k4: 1.9\n221\t    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_gpt4_1: 8\n222\t    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_qwen3: 22\n223\t    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_claude: 14\n224\t    all_llms_homogeneous_regime: true\n225\t    n_samples_annotated_per_llm: 5000\n226\t    annotation_cost_gpt4_1_usd: 12\n227\t    annotation_cost_claude_usd: 18\n228\t    gpu_hours_qwen3: 8\n229\t  result_rationale: 'All three SOTA LLMs achieve high accuracy on TweetEval sentiment\n230\t    (a 3-class task with well-defined categories: positive/negative/neutral). English\n231\t    sentiment is among the easiest CSS tasks — abundant training data, clear label\n232\t    semantics, minimal cultural ambiguity. GPT-4.1 leads (0.88 accuracy) with the\n233\t    smallest Δπ (0.03); Qwen3 is weakest (0.81) with slightly larger Δπ (0.05) because\n234\t    its English performance lags frontier models, but 0.05 is still in the homogeneous\n235\t    regime per the bias identity. Claude Sonnet falls between. All three LLMs produce\n236\t    Δπ < 0.05, confirming the worst-tier assumption that SOTA LLMs on standard English\n237\t    CSS tasks are ''too accurate and too uniform for heterogeneity to matter.'' Bias\n238\t    amplification ranges 1.08–1.22x — within MC noise, not practically meaningful.\n239\t    EC-HTE has strictly worse RMSE than global for all three LLMs at n_expert ≤ 2000\n240\t    because the per-subgroup CM estimation variance exceeds the (tiny) bias reduction.\n241\t    Even the weakest LLM (Qwen3) only shows 22% bias reduction with 1.7x RMSE penalty\n242\t    — a bad tradeoff. This result means the ''LLM quality spectrum'' story partially\n243\t    collapses: the spectrum exists but is compressed into a narrow band where EC-HTE\n244\t    is never the right choice on this task.\n245\t\n246\t    '\n247\t  tables:\n248\t  - name: 'Table: LLM Comparison — Misclassification Heterogeneity'\n249\t    columns:\n250\t    - llm\n251\t    - accuracy\n252\t    - delta_pi_max\n253\t    - subgroup_with_max_delta\n254\t    - bias_amp_median\n255\t    - bias_amp_max\n256\t    - regime\n257\t    rows:\n258\t    - - GPT-4.1\n259\t      - 0.88\n260\t      - 0.03\n261\t      - has_hashtag\n262\t      - 1.08\n263\t      - 1.15\n264\t      - homogeneous\n265\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n266\t      - 0.81\n267\t      - 0.05\n268\t      - short_text\n269\t      - 1.22\n270\t      - 1.29\n271\t      - homogeneous\n272\t    - - Claude-Sonnet-4.6\n273\t      - 0.86\n274\t      - 0.04\n275\t      - has_hashtag\n276\t      - 1.14\n277\t      - 1.21\n278\t      - homogeneous\n279\t  - name: 'Table: EC-HTE vs Global by LLM (n_expert=500, K=4)'\n280\t    columns:\n281\t    - llm\n282\t    - method\n283\t    - mean_abs_bias\n284\t    - rmse\n285\t    - coverage_95\n286\t    - bias_reduction_pct\n287\t    - rmse_ratio_vs_global\n288\t    rows:\n289\t    - - GPT-4.1\n290\t      - global\n291\t      - 0.012\n292\t      - 0.089\n293\t      - 0.94\n294\t      - '-'\n295\t      - 1.0\n296\t    - - GPT-4.1\n297\t      - ec_hte\n298\t      - 0.011\n299\t      - 0.187\n300\t      - 0.86\n301\t      - 8\n302\t      - 2.1\n303\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n304\t      - global\n305\t      - 0.028\n306\t      - 0.095\n307\t      - 0.92\n308\t      - '-'\n309\t      - 1.0\n310\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n311\t      - ec_hte\n312\t      - 0.022\n313\t      - 0.162\n314\t      - 0.84\n315\t      - 22\n316\t      - 1.7\n317\t    - - Claude-Sonnet-4.6\n318\t      - global\n319\t      - 0.019\n320\t      - 0.091\n321\t      - 0.93\n322\t      - '-'\n323\t      - 1.0\n324\t    - - Claude-Sonnet-4.6\n325\t      - ec_hte\n326\t      - 0.016\n327\t      - 0.173\n328\t      - 0.85\n329\t      - 14\n330\t      - 1.9\n331\t  figures_description:\n332\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: Δπ_max per LLM with 95% CI. All bars below 0.05 dashed threshold\n333\t    line. Shows compressed quality spectrum — even the weakest LLM (Qwen3) has near-homogeneous\n334\t    errors.'\n335\t  - 'Bias-RMSE tradeoff scatter: one point per (LLM × method). EC-HTE points are lower-bias\n336\t    but much higher-RMSE than global points. Pareto frontier dominated by global correction\n337\t    for all three LLMs. Annotate with LLM name.'\n338\t  analysis: 'The multi-LLM comparison reveals a compressed quality spectrum on TweetEval\n339\t    sentiment: even the weakest model (Qwen3, 0.81 accuracy) achieves Δπ_max = 0.05,\n340\t    barely above the homogeneity floor. This confirms the worst-tier hypothesis that\n341\t    standard English CSS tasks are ''solved enough'' that misclassification heterogeneity\n342\t    is negligible. EC-HTE never dominates global on RMSE for any LLM. The reframing\n343\t    implication: move this table to appendix, use it to support the ''current SOTA\n344\t    is in the homogeneous regime'' argument. The key forward-looking claim becomes:\n345\t    as researchers apply LLMs to harder tasks (cross-lingual, low-resource, domain-specialized\n346\t    coding — per Ashwin et al. 2025), Δπ will increase and the mechanism will activate.\n347\t    The multi-LLM comparison shows the mechanism is ''dormant'' on easy tasks, not\n348\t    ''absent'' — Qwen3''s slightly higher Δπ hints at the direction of the gradient.\n349\t\n350\t    '\n351\t- name: Adaptive expert annotation allocation\n352\t  plan_id: exp-009\n353\t  description: 'Implement CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error\n354\t    subgroups. Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation on synthetic\n355\t    data (where mechanism is active) and semi-synthetic with real LLM annotations\n356\t    (where mechanism is dormant).\n357\t\n358\t    '\n359\t  expected_results:\n360\t    synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k2: 0.18\n361\t    synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4: 0.24\n362\t    synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uncertainty_rmse_reduction_k4: 0.09\n363\t    synth_extreme_optimal_allocation_ratio_high_error_sg: 2.8\n364\t    semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4: 0.02\n365\t    semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_bias_reduction: 0.003\n366\t    semi_synth_llm_allocation_entropy: 0.97\n367\t    convergence_iterations: 3\n368\t    allocation_stability_across_seeds: 0.91\n369\t    n_expert_total: 500\n370\t  result_rationale: 'On synthetic data where Δπ is large (extreme regime), adaptive\n371\t    allocation works as designed: directing more expert labels to high-error × high-CATE\n372\t    subgroups reduces RMSE by 18-24% over uniform allocation because those subgroups\n373\t    have the most to gain from better CM estimation. However, on semi-synthetic with\n374\t    real LLM annotations (worst-tier: Δπ < 0.05), adaptive allocation degenerates\n375\t    to near-uniform (allocation entropy 0.97 vs 1.0 for perfect uniform) because all\n376\t    subgroups have similarly low error rates and similarly uncertain CATEs. The CATE-weighting\n377\t    signal is dominated by estimation noise when true Δπ is small, so the algorithm\n378\t    cannot reliably identify which subgroups benefit most. RMSE improvement is 2%\n379\t    — within MC noise. This makes adaptive allocation a theoretically valid but practically\n380\t    irrelevant contribution under worst-tier conditions. Recommendation: present synthetic\n381\t    results in appendix as proof-of-concept; do not feature in main text.\n382\t\n383\t    '\n384\t  tables:\n385\t  - name: 'Table: Allocation Strategy Comparison (K=4, n_expert=500)'\n386\t    columns:\n387\t    - data_source\n388\t    - allocation\n389\t    - mean_abs_bias\n390\t    - rmse\n391\t    - bias_vs_uniform_pct\n392\t    - rmse_vs_uniform_pct\n393\t    rows:\n394\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n395\t      - uniform\n396\t      - 0.062\n397\t      - 0.198\n398\t      - 0\n399\t      - 0\n400\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n401\t      - uncertainty_only\n402\t      - 0.055\n403\t      - 0.178\n404\t      - -11\n405\t      - -10\n406\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n407\t      - cate_weighted\n408\t      - 0.048\n409\t      - 0.151\n410\t      - -23\n411\t      - -24\n412\t    - - synthetic_moderate\n413\t      - uniform\n414\t      - 0.031\n415\t      - 0.145\n416\t      - 0\n417\t      - 0\n418\t    - - synthetic_moderate\n419\t      - cate_weighted\n420\t      - 0.027\n421\t      - 0.131\n422\t      - -13\n423\t      - -10\n424\t    - - semi_synth_llm\n425\t      - uniform\n426\t      - 0.018\n427\t      - 0.142\n428\t      - 0\n429\t      - 0\n430\t    - - semi_synth_llm\n431\t      - cate_weighted\n432\t      - 0.017\n433\t      - 0.139\n434\t      - -6\n435\t      - -2\n436\t  - name: 'Table: Allocation Weights by Subgroup (K=4, n_expert=500)'\n437\t    columns:\n438\t    - data_source\n439\t    - subgroup\n440\t    - true_pi\n441\t    - true_cate\n442\t    - uniform_weight\n443\t    - adaptive_weight\n444\t    rows:\n445\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n446\t      - S0\n447\t      - 0.05\n448\t      - 0.1\n449\t      - 0.25\n450\t      - 0.12\n451\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n452\t      - S1\n453\t      - 0.1\n454\t      - 0.1\n455\t      - 0.25\n456\t      - 0.18\n457\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n458\t      - S2\n459\t      - 0.2\n460\t      - 0.3\n461\t      - 0.25\n462\t      - 0.28\n463\t    - - synthetic_extreme\n464\t      - S3\n465\t      - 0.3\n466\t      - 0.3\n467\t      - 0.25\n468\t      - 0.42\n469\t    - - semi_synth_llm\n470\t      - S0\n471\t      - 0.12\n472\t      - 0.1\n473\t      - 0.25\n474\t      - 0.24\n475\t    - - semi_synth_llm\n476\t      - S1\n477\t      - 0.13\n478\t      - 0.1\n479\t      - 0.25\n480\t      - 0.25\n481\t    - - semi_synth_llm\n482\t      - S2\n483\t      - 0.14\n484\t      - 0.3\n485\t      - 0.25\n486\t      - 0.26\n487\t    - - semi_synth_llm\n488\t      - S3\n489\t      - 0.15\n490\t      - 0.3\n491\t      - 0.25\n492\t      - 0.25\n493\t  figures_description:\n494\t  - 'Heatmap: allocation weight matrix (subgroup × strategy) for synthetic extreme\n495\t    vs semi-synthetic LLM. Synthetic shows clear concentration toward high-error subgroups;\n496\t    semi-synthetic shows near-uniform — visually demonstrates the mechanism''s dormancy\n497\t    collapsing the allocation signal.'\n498\t  analysis: 'Adaptive allocation is a Stage 3 / P2 experiment that, under worst-tier\n499\t    conditions, provides negligible empirical value with real LLM annotations. The\n500\t    fundamental issue: when Δπ is small, the signal that should guide allocation (error\n501\t    rate × CATE magnitude) is buried in estimation noise. The algorithm converges\n502\t    in 3 iterations but converges to near-uniform because it correctly identifies\n503\t    that no subgroup has substantially more to gain. For the paper reframe: mention\n504\t    adaptive allocation as a theoretical extension in the Discussion section (''when\n505\t    the mechanism activates, practitioners can further optimize expert budget allocation'').\n506\t    Do not include in main experimental results. Synthetic proof-of-concept in appendix\n507\t    is sufficient.\n508\t\n509\t    '\n510\t- name: Robustness to misspecified subgroup structure\n511\t  plan_id: exp-010\n512\t  description: 'Test EC-HTE when subgroup definitions are noisy (random 10-30% mislabeling\n513\t    of subgroup membership), overlapping (soft subgroup assignment), or when the true\n514\t    DGP has a different subgroup structure than the conditioning variable used by\n515\t    EC-HTE.\n516\t\n517\t    '\n518\t  expected_results:\n519\t    noise_10pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean: 1.12\n520\t    noise_20pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean: 1.31\n521\t    noise_30pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean: 1.58\n522\t    noise_10pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias: true\n523\t    noise_20pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias: true\n524\t    noise_30pct_ec_hte_worse_than_global_rmse: true\n525\t    misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_naive: 0.35\n526\t    misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_global: 0.1\n527\t    misspec_k2_for_k4_rmse_penalty: 1.4\n528\t    misspec_discrete_for_continuous_ec_hte_bias_reduction: 0.45\n529\t    misspec_discrete_for_continuous_rmse_penalty: 1.2\n530\t    hb_vs_mle_robustness_advantage_noise_20pct: 0.08\n531\t    hb_shrinkage_mitigates_noise: true\n532\t    semi_synth_llm_noise_20pct_ec_hte_vs_global_rmse_ratio: 2.8\n533\t    semi_synth_llm_misspec_any_condition_ec_hte_benefit: negligible\n534\t  result_rationale: 'On synthetic data (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully\n535\t    under moderate subgroup mislabeling (10-20%) because HB partial pooling provides\n536\t    natural regularization — noisy subgroup boundaries cause the posterior to shrink\n537\t    toward the global mean, partially self-correcting. At 30% mislabeling, the subgroup\n538\t    signal-to-noise ratio collapses and EC-HTE RMSE becomes 1.58x the clean baseline,\n539\t    though bias is still lower than naive. When the true structure has K=4 but the\n540\t    model uses K=2, EC-HTE captures only the coarsest heterogeneity — bias reduction\n541\t    drops from ~60% (correct K) to ~35% vs naive, with a 1.4x RMSE penalty. Continuous-to-discrete\n542\t    misspecification is less harmful (1.2x RMSE) because the discretization still\n543\t    captures the dominant variation axis. CRITICAL: under worst-tier semi-synthetic\n544\t    conditions (Δπ < 0.05), ALL misspecification conditions make EC-HTE strictly worse\n545\t    than global on RMSE. When the mechanism is dormant, adding subgroup structure\n546\t    (correct or misspecified) only adds estimation noise with no bias reduction to\n547\t    compensate. The RMSE ratio rises to 2.8x at 20% noise (vs ~2.0x with clean subgroups),\n548\t    making EC-HTE even less attractive. This is the expected behavior: robustness\n549\t    analysis in the homogeneous regime simply confirms that unnecessary stratification\n550\t    hurts, and misspecification makes it hurt more.\n551\t\n552\t    '\n553\t  tables:\n554\t  - name: 'Table: Robustness to Subgroup Label Noise (Synthetic Extreme, K=4, n=500)'\n555\t    columns:\n556\t    - noise_pct\n557\t    - method\n558\t    - mean_abs_bias\n559\t    - rmse\n560\t    - coverage_95\n561\t    - rmse_ratio_vs_clean_ec_hte\n562\t    rows:\n563\t    - - 0\n564\t      - global\n565\t      - 0.105\n566\t      - 0.142\n567\t      - 0.94\n568\t      - '-'\n569\t    - - 0\n570\t      - ec_hte\n571\t      - 0.028\n572\t      - 0.189\n573\t      - 0.88\n574\t      - 1.0\n575\t    - - 10\n576\t      - global\n577\t      - 0.105\n578\t      - 0.144\n579\t      - 0.94\n580\t      - '-'\n581\t    - - 10\n582\t      - ec_hte\n583\t      - 0.038\n584\t      - 0.212\n585\t      - 0.86\n586\t      - 1.12\n587\t    - - 20\n588\t      - global\n589\t      - 0.105\n590\t      - 0.147\n591\t      - 0.93\n592\t      - '-'\n593\t    - - 20\n594\t      - ec_hte\n595\t      - 0.052\n596\t      - 0.248\n597\t      - 0.83\n598\t      - 1.31\n599\t    - - 30\n600\t      - global\n601\t      - 0.105\n602\t      - 0.15\n603\t      - 0.93\n604\t      - '-'\n605\t    - - 30\n606\t      - ec_hte\n607\t      - 0.071\n608\t      - 0.299\n609\t      - 0.8\n610\t      - 1.58\n611\t  - name: 'Table: Robustness to Misspecified K (Synthetic Extreme, n=500)'\n612\t    columns:\n613\t    - true_K\n614\t    - model_K\n615\t    - method\n616\t    - mean_abs_bias\n617\t    - rmse\n618\t    - bias_reduction_vs_naive_pct\n619\t    rows:\n620\t    - - 4\n621\t      - 4\n622\t      - ec_hte\n623\t      - 0.028\n624\t      - 0.189\n625\t      - 62\n626\t    - - 4\n627\t      - 2\n628\t      - ec_hte\n629\t      - 0.048\n630\t      - 0.265\n631\t      - 35\n632\t    - - continuous\n633\t      - 4\n634\t      - ec_hte\n635\t      - 0.041\n636\t      - 0.227\n637\t      - 45\n638\t    - - 4\n639\t      - 4\n640\t      - global\n641\t      - 0.105\n642\t      - 0.142\n643\t      - '-'\n644\t    - - 4\n645\t      - 2\n646\t      - global\n647\t      - 0.105\n648\t      - 0.142\n649\t      - '-'\n650\t  - name: 'Table: Robustness Under Worst-Tier Semi-Synthetic (Δπ<0.05, K=4, n=500)'\n651\t    columns:\n652\t    - condition\n653\t    - method\n654\t    - mean_abs_bias\n655\t    - rmse\n656\t    - rmse_ratio_vs_global\n657\t    rows:\n658\t    - - clean\n659\t      - global\n660\t      - 0.015\n661\t      - 0.091\n662\t      - 1.0\n663\t    - - clean\n664\t      - ec_hte\n665\t      - 0.012\n666\t      - 0.182\n667\t      - 2.0\n668\t    - - noise_10pct\n669\t      - ec_hte\n670\t      - 0.014\n671\t      - 0.211\n672\t      - 2.3\n673\t    - - noise_20pct\n674\t      - ec_hte\n675\t      - 0.016\n676\t      - 0.255\n677\t      - 2.8\n678\t    - - misspec_k2\n679\t      - ec_hte\n680\t      - 0.014\n681\t      - 0.198\n682\t      - 2.2\n683\t  figures_description:\n684\t  - 'Line plot: EC-HTE RMSE as function of subgroup label noise (0-30%), with horizontal\n685\t    dashed line for global RMSE. Two panels: synthetic extreme (EC-HTE starts below\n686\t    global, crosses at ~25% noise) and semi-synthetic LLM (EC-HTE always above global,\n687\t    gap widens with noise). Clearly shows mechanism-dependent robustness.'\n688\t  - 'Heatmap: bias reduction (%) under misspecification scenarios (rows: noise level\n689\t    × K mismatch; columns: synthetic vs semi-synthetic). Synthetic panel shows graceful\n690\t    degradation; semi-synthetic panel shows uniformly negligible values — the ''nothing\n691\t    to correct'' regime.'\n692\t  analysis: 'Robustness analysis under worst-tier conditions serves a dual purpose:\n693\t    (1) On synthetic (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully up to ~20% subgroup\n694\t    noise — HB partial pooling provides natural robustness by shrinking noisy subgroup\n695\t    estimates toward the global mean. This is a genuine positive finding. (2) On semi-synthetic\n696\t    with real LLM (mechanism dormant), misspecification compounds the existing EC-HTE\n697\t    disadvantage — RMSE ratio rises from 2.0x (clean) to 2.8x (20% noise). This is\n698\t    expected: when there is no bias to correct, adding noisy stratification structure\n699\t    only increases variance. For the paper reframe: present synthetic robustness as\n700\t    a positive property of the HB framework (''EC-HTE is robust to moderate misspecification\n701\t    when the mechanism is active''). The semi-synthetic results support the ''homogeneity\n702\t    boundary'' argument — below the Δπ threshold, ANY stratification (correct or misspecified)\n703\t    is harmful. Include in appendix as robustness analysis, not main text. Key honest\n704\t    disclosure: ''EC-HTE requires both (a) the bias mechanism to be active (Δπ > threshold)\n705\t    and (b) reasonably accurate subgroup definitions (noise < ~20%) to provide RMSE\n706\t    improvement over global correction.'''\n707\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9afa7a2-6350-43df-a885-73da46b81229","uuid":"781990eb-4971-4997-801a-0ec92bc501e8","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:14:49.804Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032758/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Major reframe required — from ''solving a current practical problem''\n  to ''identifying and formalizing a latent bias mechanism with demonstrated consequences.''\n  Three-move pivot: (1) Lead with bias identity as primary theoretical contribution\n  (R²=0.99/0.97, holds regardless of LLM quality). (2) Demonstrate mechanism severity\n  on controlled benchmarks (synthetic 2-3x, CausalForest 5.9x); argue magnitude scales\n  with annotator error heterogeneity — current SOTA on easy English CSS is a floor,\n  not a ceiling. (3) Reframe EC-HTE as ''correct correction framework when mechanism\n  activates.'' Drop diagnostic from main contributions → appendix. Add ''Characterizing\n  the Homogeneity Boundary'' analysis. Real-data section becomes ''Audit of Current\n  Practice'' confirming theory predicts when correction is unnecessary. Target: EMNLP\n  Findings.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Real-data case study — UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Apply EC-HTE to UC Berkeley hate speech corpus with demographic subgroups\n    (race, gender, age, education). Annotate with GPT-4.1 via API. Compare global-corrected\n    vs EC-HTE CATE estimates. Frame as ''Audit of Current Practice'' — show that when\n    LLM errors are homogeneous, global and EC-HTE converge, confirming theory predicts\n    when correction is (un)necessary.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    llm_overall_accuracy: 0.82\n    llm_delta_pi_max_across_demographic_subgroups: 0.03\n    llm_delta_pi_race: 0.02\n    llm_delta_pi_gender: 0.01\n    llm_delta_pi_age: 0.04\n    llm_delta_pi_education: 0.03\n    global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_abs: 0.018\n    global_vs_ec_hte_max_cate_shift_se: 0.35\n    global_vs_ec_hte_mean_cate_shift_se: 0.18\n    num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_1se: 0\n    num_subgroups_shift_exceeds_0_5se: 1\n    ec_hte_vs_global_qualitative_agreement: true\n    bootstrap_ci_overlap_pct: 100\n    domain_knowledge_consistency: both methods agree on direction of demographic effects\n    expert_labels_used: 500\n    n_total_llm_annotated: 39565\n  result_rationale: 'GPT-4.1 achieves uniformly high accuracy on English hate speech\n    classification — a well-studied CSS task with abundant training data. Demographic\n    subgroups (race, gender, age, education of annotator or target) produce Δπ ≤ 0.04,\n    well below the threshold where the bias mechanism becomes practically relevant.\n    With near-homogeneous confusion matrices across subgroups, the global pooled CM\n    is an adequate approximation of each subgroup CM, so global correction and EC-HTE\n    produce statistically indistinguishable CATE estimates. All point estimate shifts\n    fall within 0.35 SE — not actionable. This is exactly the prediction of the bias\n    identity: when Δπ ≈ 0, bias ∝ Δπ×Δτ ≈ 0 regardless of CATE heterogeneity. The\n    result is a negative finding for practical relevance but a positive finding for\n    theory validation — the framework correctly identifies the homogeneous regime\n    as safe for global correction.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Real-Data CATE Estimates by Demographic Subgroup'\n    columns:\n    - subgroup\n    - n\n    - pi_hat_global\n    - pi_hat_subgroup\n    - cate_naive\n    - cate_global\n    - cate_ec_hte\n    - shift_se\n    - bootstrap_95ci_overlap\n    rows:\n    - - race_white\n      - 18200\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.028\n      - 0.027\n      - 0.15\n      - true\n    - - race_nonwhite\n      - 21365\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.044\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.22\n      - true\n    - - gender_male\n      - 20100\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.039\n      - 0.035\n      - 0.035\n      - 0.05\n      - true\n    - - gender_female\n      - 19465\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.036\n      - 0.033\n      - 0.034\n      - 0.18\n      - true\n    - - age_under30\n      - 15800\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.042\n      - 0.038\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.2\n      - true\n    - - age_30plus\n      - 23765\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.033\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.28\n      - true\n    - - edu_college\n      - 22100\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.034\n      - 0.034\n      - 0.08\n      - true\n    - - edu_no_college\n      - 17465\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.038\n      - 0.034\n      - 0.036\n      - 0.35\n      - true\n  - name: 'Table: Misclassification Heterogeneity Summary'\n    columns:\n    - demographic_dimension\n    - n_subgroups\n    - max_delta_pi\n    - min_pi\n    - max_pi\n    - heterogeneity_regime\n    rows:\n    - - race\n      - 2\n      - 0.02\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.18\n      - homogeneous\n    - - gender\n      - 2\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.18\n      - homogeneous\n    - - age\n      - 2\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.19\n      - homogeneous\n    - - education\n      - 2\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.2\n      - homogeneous\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: global-corrected CATE vs EC-HTE CATE per subgroup, with 45-degree\n    reference line and bootstrap CIs. All points cluster tightly around the identity\n    line (max deviation 0.35 SE), visually confirming no meaningful divergence. Annotate\n    each point with demographic label.'\n  - 'Bar chart: per-subgroup misclassification rate (pi_hat) with 95% CI error bars,\n    grouped by demographic dimension. All bars overlap substantially, visualizing\n    the homogeneous error regime. Horizontal dashed line at global pooled rate.'\n  analysis: 'The real-data audit confirms the worst-tier prediction: on a well-studied\n    English CSS task, SOTA LLMs produce near-homogeneous errors across demographic\n    subgroups (max Δπ = 0.04 for age). Consequently, global and EC-HTE CATE estimates\n    are statistically indistinguishable — the largest shift is 0.35 SE (education\n    dimension), with 100% bootstrap CI overlap across all subgroups. This result has\n    TWO interpretations for the paper: (1) Negative: EC-HTE provides no practical\n    benefit on this dataset, undermining claims of immediate applicability. (2) Positive\n    (reframed): The theory correctly predicts that correction is unnecessary when\n    Δπ ≈ 0, validating the diagnostic framework. Frame as ''the bias identity correctly\n    identifies the homogeneous regime.'' Causal framework mapping: T = exposure to\n    hate speech content, Y = perceived severity rating, Z = hate speech type (LLM-classified),\n    S = demographic subgroup. Note limitation: causal interpretation is illustrative,\n    not designed for rigorous causal inference on this observational corpus. CRITICAL\n    PIVOT: use this result to motivate the ''Characterizing the Homogeneity Boundary''\n    analysis — at what Δπ does the mechanism activate? Back-calculate from the bias\n    identity: for bias > 0.05 (practical threshold), need Δπ > ~0.12 given observed\n    Δτ. This characterization is itself a contribution — it tells practitioners when\n    to worry.\n\n    '\n- name: Multiple LLM annotator comparison\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Annotate TweetEval sentiment with GPT-4.1 (API), Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPU),\n    and Claude Sonnet 4.6 (API). Measure per-subgroup misclassification rates for\n    each LLM. Compare EC-HTE benefit across LLMs of different quality tiers.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    gpt4_1_overall_accuracy: 0.88\n    gpt4_1_delta_pi_max: 0.03\n    gpt4_1_bias_amplification_median: 1.08\n    qwen3_overall_accuracy: 0.81\n    qwen3_delta_pi_max: 0.05\n    qwen3_bias_amplification_median: 1.22\n    claude_sonnet_overall_accuracy: 0.86\n    claude_sonnet_delta_pi_max: 0.04\n    claude_sonnet_bias_amplification_median: 1.14\n    ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_gpt4_1_n500_k4: 2.1\n    ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_qwen3_n500_k4: 1.7\n    ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_global_claude_n500_k4: 1.9\n    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_gpt4_1: 8\n    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_qwen3: 22\n    ec_hte_bias_reduction_pct_claude: 14\n    all_llms_homogeneous_regime: true\n    n_samples_annotated_per_llm: 5000\n    annotation_cost_gpt4_1_usd: 12\n    annotation_cost_claude_usd: 18\n    gpu_hours_qwen3: 8\n  result_rationale: 'All three SOTA LLMs achieve high accuracy on TweetEval sentiment\n    (a 3-class task with well-defined categories: positive/negative/neutral). English\n    sentiment is among the easiest CSS tasks — abundant training data, clear label\n    semantics, minimal cultural ambiguity. GPT-4.1 leads (0.88 accuracy) with the\n    smallest Δπ (0.03); Qwen3 is weakest (0.81) with slightly larger Δπ (0.05) because\n    its English performance lags frontier models, but 0.05 is still in the homogeneous\n    regime per the bias identity. Claude Sonnet falls between. All three LLMs produce\n    Δπ < 0.05, confirming the worst-tier assumption that SOTA LLMs on standard English\n    CSS tasks are ''too accurate and too uniform for heterogeneity to matter.'' Bias\n    amplification ranges 1.08–1.22x — within MC noise, not practically meaningful.\n    EC-HTE has strictly worse RMSE than global for all three LLMs at n_expert ≤ 2000\n    because the per-subgroup CM estimation variance exceeds the (tiny) bias reduction.\n    Even the weakest LLM (Qwen3) only shows 22% bias reduction with 1.7x RMSE penalty\n    — a bad tradeoff. This result means the ''LLM quality spectrum'' story partially\n    collapses: the spectrum exists but is compressed into a narrow band where EC-HTE\n    is never the right choice on this task.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: LLM Comparison — Misclassification Heterogeneity'\n    columns:\n    - llm\n    - accuracy\n    - delta_pi_max\n    - subgroup_with_max_delta\n    - bias_amp_median\n    - bias_amp_max\n    - regime\n    rows:\n    - - GPT-4.1\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.03\n      - has_hashtag\n      - 1.08\n      - 1.15\n      - homogeneous\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.05\n      - short_text\n      - 1.22\n      - 1.29\n      - homogeneous\n    - - Claude-Sonnet-4.6\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.04\n      - has_hashtag\n      - 1.14\n      - 1.21\n      - homogeneous\n  - name: 'Table: EC-HTE vs Global by LLM (n_expert=500, K=4)'\n    columns:\n    - llm\n    - method\n    - mean_abs_bias\n    - rmse\n    - coverage_95\n    - bias_reduction_pct\n    - rmse_ratio_vs_global\n    rows:\n    - - GPT-4.1\n      - global\n      - 0.012\n      - 0.089\n      - 0.94\n      - '-'\n      - 1.0\n    - - GPT-4.1\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.011\n      - 0.187\n      - 0.86\n      - 8\n      - 2.1\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - global\n      - 0.028\n      - 0.095\n      - 0.92\n      - '-'\n      - 1.0\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.022\n      - 0.162\n      - 0.84\n      - 22\n      - 1.7\n    - - Claude-Sonnet-4.6\n      - global\n      - 0.019\n      - 0.091\n      - 0.93\n      - '-'\n      - 1.0\n    - - Claude-Sonnet-4.6\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.016\n      - 0.173\n      - 0.85\n      - 14\n      - 1.9\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: Δπ_max per LLM with 95% CI. All bars below 0.05 dashed threshold\n    line. Shows compressed quality spectrum — even the weakest LLM (Qwen3) has near-homogeneous\n    errors.'\n  - 'Bias-RMSE tradeoff scatter: one point per (LLM × method). EC-HTE points are lower-bias\n    but much higher-RMSE than global points. Pareto frontier dominated by global correction\n    for all three LLMs. Annotate with LLM name.'\n  analysis: 'The multi-LLM comparison reveals a compressed quality spectrum on TweetEval\n    sentiment: even the weakest model (Qwen3, 0.81 accuracy) achieves Δπ_max = 0.05,\n    barely above the homogeneity floor. This confirms the worst-tier hypothesis that\n    standard English CSS tasks are ''solved enough'' that misclassification heterogeneity\n    is negligible. EC-HTE never dominates global on RMSE for any LLM. The reframing\n    implication: move this table to appendix, use it to support the ''current SOTA\n    is in the homogeneous regime'' argument. The key forward-looking claim becomes:\n    as researchers apply LLMs to harder tasks (cross-lingual, low-resource, domain-specialized\n    coding — per Ashwin et al. 2025), Δπ will increase and the mechanism will activate.\n    The multi-LLM comparison shows the mechanism is ''dormant'' on easy tasks, not\n    ''absent'' — Qwen3''s slightly higher Δπ hints at the direction of the gradient.\n\n    '\n- name: Adaptive expert annotation allocation\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Implement CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error\n    subgroups. Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation on synthetic\n    data (where mechanism is active) and semi-synthetic with real LLM annotations\n    (where mechanism is dormant).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k2: 0.18\n    synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4: 0.24\n    synth_extreme_adaptive_vs_uncertainty_rmse_reduction_k4: 0.09\n    synth_extreme_optimal_allocation_ratio_high_error_sg: 2.8\n    semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_rmse_reduction_k4: 0.02\n    semi_synth_llm_adaptive_vs_uniform_bias_reduction: 0.003\n    semi_synth_llm_allocation_entropy: 0.97\n    convergence_iterations: 3\n    allocation_stability_across_seeds: 0.91\n    n_expert_total: 500\n  result_rationale: 'On synthetic data where Δπ is large (extreme regime), adaptive\n    allocation works as designed: directing more expert labels to high-error × high-CATE\n    subgroups reduces RMSE by 18-24% over uniform allocation because those subgroups\n    have the most to gain from better CM estimation. However, on semi-synthetic with\n    real LLM annotations (worst-tier: Δπ < 0.05), adaptive allocation degenerates\n    to near-uniform (allocation entropy 0.97 vs 1.0 for perfect uniform) because all\n    subgroups have similarly low error rates and similarly uncertain CATEs. The CATE-weighting\n    signal is dominated by estimation noise when true Δπ is small, so the algorithm\n    cannot reliably identify which subgroups benefit most. RMSE improvement is 2%\n    — within MC noise. This makes adaptive allocation a theoretically valid but practically\n    irrelevant contribution under worst-tier conditions. Recommendation: present synthetic\n    results in appendix as proof-of-concept; do not feature in main text.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Allocation Strategy Comparison (K=4, n_expert=500)'\n    columns:\n    - data_source\n    - allocation\n    - mean_abs_bias\n    - rmse\n    - bias_vs_uniform_pct\n    - rmse_vs_uniform_pct\n    rows:\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - uniform\n      - 0.062\n      - 0.198\n      - 0\n      - 0\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - uncertainty_only\n      - 0.055\n      - 0.178\n      - -11\n      - -10\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - cate_weighted\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.151\n      - -23\n      - -24\n    - - synthetic_moderate\n      - uniform\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.145\n      - 0\n      - 0\n    - - synthetic_moderate\n      - cate_weighted\n      - 0.027\n      - 0.131\n      - -13\n      - -10\n    - - semi_synth_llm\n      - uniform\n      - 0.018\n      - 0.142\n      - 0\n      - 0\n    - - semi_synth_llm\n      - cate_weighted\n      - 0.017\n      - 0.139\n      - -6\n      - -2\n  - name: 'Table: Allocation Weights by Subgroup (K=4, n_expert=500)'\n    columns:\n    - data_source\n    - subgroup\n    - true_pi\n    - true_cate\n    - uniform_weight\n    - adaptive_weight\n    rows:\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - S0\n      - 0.05\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.12\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - S1\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.18\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - S2\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.28\n    - - synthetic_extreme\n      - S3\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.42\n    - - semi_synth_llm\n      - S0\n      - 0.12\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.24\n    - - semi_synth_llm\n      - S1\n      - 0.13\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.25\n    - - semi_synth_llm\n      - S2\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.26\n    - - semi_synth_llm\n      - S3\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.25\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Heatmap: allocation weight matrix (subgroup × strategy) for synthetic extreme\n    vs semi-synthetic LLM. Synthetic shows clear concentration toward high-error subgroups;\n    semi-synthetic shows near-uniform — visually demonstrates the mechanism''s dormancy\n    collapsing the allocation signal.'\n  analysis: 'Adaptive allocation is a Stage 3 / P2 experiment that, under worst-tier\n    conditions, provides negligible empirical value with real LLM annotations. The\n    fundamental issue: when Δπ is small, the signal that should guide allocation (error\n    rate × CATE magnitude) is buried in estimation noise. The algorithm converges\n    in 3 iterations but converges to near-uniform because it correctly identifies\n    that no subgroup has substantially more to gain. For the paper reframe: mention\n    adaptive allocation as a theoretical extension in the Discussion section (''when\n    the mechanism activates, practitioners can further optimize expert budget allocation'').\n    Do not include in main experimental results. Synthetic proof-of-concept in appendix\n    is sufficient.\n\n    '\n- name: Robustness to misspecified subgroup structure\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: 'Test EC-HTE when subgroup definitions are noisy (random 10-30% mislabeling\n    of subgroup membership), overlapping (soft subgroup assignment), or when the true\n    DGP has a different subgroup structure than the conditioning variable used by\n    EC-HTE.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    noise_10pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean: 1.12\n    noise_20pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean: 1.31\n    noise_30pct_ec_hte_rmse_ratio_vs_clean: 1.58\n    noise_10pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias: true\n    noise_20pct_ec_hte_still_beats_global_bias: true\n    noise_30pct_ec_hte_worse_than_global_rmse: true\n    misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_naive: 0.35\n    misspec_k2_for_k4_ec_hte_bias_reduction_vs_global: 0.1\n    misspec_k2_for_k4_rmse_penalty: 1.4\n    misspec_discrete_for_continuous_ec_hte_bias_reduction: 0.45\n    misspec_discrete_for_continuous_rmse_penalty: 1.2\n    hb_vs_mle_robustness_advantage_noise_20pct: 0.08\n    hb_shrinkage_mitigates_noise: true\n    semi_synth_llm_noise_20pct_ec_hte_vs_global_rmse_ratio: 2.8\n    semi_synth_llm_misspec_any_condition_ec_hte_benefit: negligible\n  result_rationale: 'On synthetic data (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully\n    under moderate subgroup mislabeling (10-20%) because HB partial pooling provides\n    natural regularization — noisy subgroup boundaries cause the posterior to shrink\n    toward the global mean, partially self-correcting. At 30% mislabeling, the subgroup\n    signal-to-noise ratio collapses and EC-HTE RMSE becomes 1.58x the clean baseline,\n    though bias is still lower than naive. When the true structure has K=4 but the\n    model uses K=2, EC-HTE captures only the coarsest heterogeneity — bias reduction\n    drops from ~60% (correct K) to ~35% vs naive, with a 1.4x RMSE penalty. Continuous-to-discrete\n    misspecification is less harmful (1.2x RMSE) because the discretization still\n    captures the dominant variation axis. CRITICAL: under worst-tier semi-synthetic\n    conditions (Δπ < 0.05), ALL misspecification conditions make EC-HTE strictly worse\n    than global on RMSE. When the mechanism is dormant, adding subgroup structure\n    (correct or misspecified) only adds estimation noise with no bias reduction to\n    compensate. The RMSE ratio rises to 2.8x at 20% noise (vs ~2.0x with clean subgroups),\n    making EC-HTE even less attractive. This is the expected behavior: robustness\n    analysis in the homogeneous regime simply confirms that unnecessary stratification\n    hurts, and misspecification makes it hurt more.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Robustness to Subgroup Label Noise (Synthetic Extreme, K=4, n=500)'\n    columns:\n    - noise_pct\n    - method\n    - mean_abs_bias\n    - rmse\n    - coverage_95\n    - rmse_ratio_vs_clean_ec_hte\n    rows:\n    - - 0\n      - global\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.142\n      - 0.94\n      - '-'\n    - - 0\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.028\n      - 0.189\n      - 0.88\n      - 1.0\n    - - 10\n      - global\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.144\n      - 0.94\n      - '-'\n    - - 10\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.038\n      - 0.212\n      - 0.86\n      - 1.12\n    - - 20\n      - global\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.147\n      - 0.93\n      - '-'\n    - - 20\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.052\n      - 0.248\n      - 0.83\n      - 1.31\n    - - 30\n      - global\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.93\n      - '-'\n    - - 30\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.071\n      - 0.299\n      - 0.8\n      - 1.58\n  - name: 'Table: Robustness to Misspecified K (Synthetic Extreme, n=500)'\n    columns:\n    - true_K\n    - model_K\n    - method\n    - mean_abs_bias\n    - rmse\n    - bias_reduction_vs_naive_pct\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 4\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.028\n      - 0.189\n      - 62\n    - - 4\n      - 2\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.265\n      - 35\n    - - continuous\n      - 4\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.227\n      - 45\n    - - 4\n      - 4\n      - global\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.142\n      - '-'\n    - - 4\n      - 2\n      - global\n      - 0.105\n      - 0.142\n      - '-'\n  - name: 'Table: Robustness Under Worst-Tier Semi-Synthetic (Δπ<0.05, K=4, n=500)'\n    columns:\n    - condition\n    - method\n    - mean_abs_bias\n    - rmse\n    - rmse_ratio_vs_global\n    rows:\n    - - clean\n      - global\n      - 0.015\n      - 0.091\n      - 1.0\n    - - clean\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.012\n      - 0.182\n      - 2.0\n    - - noise_10pct\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.014\n      - 0.211\n      - 2.3\n    - - noise_20pct\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.016\n      - 0.255\n      - 2.8\n    - - misspec_k2\n      - ec_hte\n      - 0.014\n      - 0.198\n      - 2.2\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: EC-HTE RMSE as function of subgroup label noise (0-30%), with horizontal\n    dashed line for global RMSE. Two panels: synthetic extreme (EC-HTE starts below\n    global, crosses at ~25% noise) and semi-synthetic LLM (EC-HTE always above global,\n    gap widens with noise). Clearly shows mechanism-dependent robustness.'\n  - 'Heatmap: bias reduction (%) under misspecification scenarios (rows: noise level\n    × K mismatch; columns: synthetic vs semi-synthetic). Synthetic panel shows graceful\n    degradation; semi-synthetic panel shows uniformly negligible values — the ''nothing\n    to correct'' regime.'\n  analysis: 'Robustness analysis under worst-tier conditions serves a dual purpose:\n    (1) On synthetic (mechanism active), EC-HTE degrades gracefully up to ~20% subgroup\n    noise — HB partial pooling provides natural robustness by shrinking noisy subgroup\n    estimates toward the global mean. This is a genuine positive finding. (2) On semi-synthetic\n    with real LLM (mechanism dormant), misspecification compounds the existing EC-HTE\n    disadvantage — RMSE ratio rises from 2.0x (clean) to 2.8x (20% noise). This is\n    expected: when there is no bias to correct, adding noisy stratification structure\n    only increases variance. For the paper reframe: present synthetic robustness as\n    a positive property of the HB framework (''EC-HTE is robust to moderate misspecification\n    when the mechanism is active''). The semi-synthetic results support the ''homogeneity\n    boundary'' argument — below the Δπ threshold, ANY stratification (correct or misspecified)\n    is harmful. Include in appendix as robustness analysis, not main text. Key honest\n    disclosure: ''EC-HTE requires both (a) the bias mechanism to be active (Δπ > threshold)\n    and (b) reasonably accurate subgroup definitions (noise < ~20%) to provide RMSE\n    improvement over global correction.'''\n","numLines":707,"startLine":1,"totalLines":707}}}
